 \section{Procedure}
 Participants will perform an agent-guided object-search task while seated on a swivel chair, with multiple colored geometric objects scattered across eight surrounding vertical planes. Although the application knows the target identity, the participant will not receive a color-and-shape description before searching. Instead, the agent will guide the participant toward the target wall and then refine the direction horizontally, vertically or diagonally. The participant will select an object using the right-controller ray and trigger; looking at an object, receiving a gaze confirmation or pointing at it will not itself complete the trial.
 
-Each trial will begin with the participant looking at a readiness cross. Once readiness is established, an opening directional cue will play while the objects remain hidden, and object reveal will then start active search timing. A correct trigger selection will stop timing and produce success feedback, whereas an incorrect selection will be counted and search will continue. This sequence separates readiness and opening guidance from the timed search interval while retaining their timestamps for analysis.
+Each trial will begin with the participant looking at a readiness cross. Once readiness is established, an opening directional cue will play while the objects remain hidden, and object reveal will then start active search timing. A correct trigger selection before the 15-second active-search deadline stops timing and produces success feedback; an incorrect selection is counted and search continues within that same limit. This sequence separates readiness and opening guidance from the timed search interval while retaining their timestamps for analysis.
+
+If the target has not been selected after 15 seconds of active search, the trial ends as a timeout. The application hides the objects, cancels pending spatial hints, and plays a prepared encouragement in the assigned voice, beginning with ``Time up,'' before presenting the next readiness cross. For example, the agent may say, ``Time up. Let's shake that round off and start the next.'' Separate wording introduces the block break or acknowledges the final trial. Timeout feedback is outside the search interval and does not imply a correct selection. The same deadline and phrase set apply to both voice conditions and to the single practice trial; a timeout advances the scheduled attempt without adding a replacement. Explicit pauses suspend the clock, whereas tracking loss alone does not. The 15-second limit is a researcher-selected protocol parameter introduced on October 23, 2026, rather than a threshold established by the cited studies.
 
 Before participant use, consent materials must be reconciled with ElevenLabs voice processing and Jev cloud decisions, including the data transmitted, retention, access, and deletion arrangements. This reconciliation remains a requirement before participant recruitment. After consent, participants will complete background questions and baseline symptom ratings. They will then choose a neutral voice, rate auditioned voices, record a standard passage in their natural voice for synthesis, and confirm comfortable, clear audio for each condition. The retained English recording passage is reproduced in Appendix~\ref{app:instruments}, although its match to the frozen build and typical recording duration remain to be verified. Preferred gender presentation will be chosen explicitly rather than inferred from participant gender.
 
